/**
 * HOWL Chat — Tutorial Data: Model Settings (Exhaustive)
 *
 * Every slider, number, toggle, select, and effect documented.
 *
 * @module tutorials-data-model
 * @version 1.0.0
 */

'use strict';

window.TUTORIALS_DATA_MODEL = {
    id: 'model-settings',
    title: 'Model Settings',
    topics: [
        {
            id: 'model-overview',
            title: 'Model Settings Overview',
            content: "# Model Settings Overview\n\nModel settings control **how the LLM backend runs**, including hardware utilization, memory management, context handling, and prompt formatting. These are system-level settings that affect performance, memory usage, and generation quality.\n\n## Where to Find Them\n\n**Settings → Model Settings** (topbar menu) or **File → Advanced**.\n\n## Categories\n\n- **Core Model Settings**: Model family, variant, threads, batch size, GPU offload\n- **Performance Settings**: KV cache, rope scaling, tensor split\n- **Memory & Context Settings**: Context window, rollover mode, memory integration\n- **Prompt & Template Settings**: Template selection, system prompt, stop sequences\n\n<info>\nModel settings affect ALL chats using the loaded model. Changes apply immediately to new generations.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'model-family',
            title: 'Model Family',
            content: "# Model Family\n\n**Select**: Auto, LLaMA, Mistral, Phi, Qwen, LFM, Gemma | **Default**: Auto\n\nModel Family tells the backend which model architecture is being used. This affects default tokenizer, prompt template, context handling, and rope scaling behavior.\n\n## How It Works\n\nThe backend uses the family setting to:\n- Choose the correct prompt template format\n- Apply appropriate rope scaling defaults\n- Set safe defaults for KV cache and context size\n- Handle tokenizer-specific quirks\n\n## What the Values Mean\n\n| Value | Use For |\n|-------|---------|\n| **Auto** | Recommended. Detected from GGUF metadata. |\n| **LLaMA** | Meta's LLaMA/LLaMA 2/LLaMA 3 models. |\n| **Mistral** | Mistral 7B, Mixtral 8x7B, and derivatives. |\n| **Phi** | Microsoft's Phi-2/Phi-3 small models. |\n| **Qwen** | Alibaba's Qwen series. |\n| **LFM** | LLaMA For Math and reasoning models. |\n| **Gemma** | Google's Gemma/Gemma 2 models. |\n\n## When to Override\n\n- **Keep Auto**: For most models. The GGUF metadata is usually correct.\n- **Override**: If the model was converted incorrectly and metadata is missing or wrong.\n- **Override**: If you're using a custom fine-tune that inherits from a base architecture but has different metadata.\n\n<warning>\nIncorrect family selection can cause the model to produce garbage or fail to load. Only override if you know the architecture is wrong.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'model-variant',
            title: 'Model Variant',
            content: "# Model Variant\n\n**Select**: Auto, 1.2B, 3B, 7B, 13B, 70B | **Default**: Auto\n\nModel Variant indicates the parameter count of the model. This helps the backend set safe defaults for threads, batch size, and KV cache based on the model's size and memory requirements.\n\n## How It Works\n\nSmaller models need less memory and can run with smaller batch sizes. Larger models need more aggressive GPU offloading and larger KV caches.\n\n## What the Values Mean\n\n| Value | Parameter Count | Typical VRAM/RAM |\n|-------|-----------------|------------------|\n| **1.2B** | 1.2 billion | ~2 GB VRAM or ~4 GB RAM |\n| **3B** | 3 billion | ~4 GB VRAM or ~8 GB RAM |\n| **7B** | 7 billion | ~6 GB VRAM or ~14 GB RAM |\n| **13B** | 13 billion | ~10 GB VRAM or ~26 GB RAM |\n| **70B** | 70 billion | ~40 GB VRAM (multi-GPU) or ~140 GB RAM |\n\n## When to Override\n\n- **Keep Auto**: For most models. Detected from GGUF metadata.\n- **Override**: If the model is a quantized variant (e.g., a 7B model quantized to 4-bit). The variant selector doesn't account for quantization level.\n- **Override**: If you're testing performance and want to force different defaults.\n\n<info>\nVariant affects DEFAULTS only. You can still manually override threads, batch size, and GPU layers regardless of the variant setting.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'thread-count',
            title: 'Thread Count',
            content: "# Thread Count\n\n**Slider**: 1 to 32 | **Step**: 1 | **Default**: 11 (auto-detected as num_cores - 1)\n\nThread Count controls how many CPU threads the backend uses for parallel inference. More threads = faster generation but higher CPU usage.\n\n## How It Works\n\nThe model computation is split across threads. Each thread processes a portion of the matrix multiplication operations.\n\n## What the Numbers Mean\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **1** | Single-threaded. Slowest but lowest CPU usage. |\n| **4** | Low-end quad-core CPU. Moderate speed. |\n| **8** | 8-core CPU. Good balance. |\n| **11** | Default (12-core CPU). Recommended for most users. |\n| **16** | High-end CPU. Faster but may hit diminishing returns. |\n| **32** | Maximum. For 32+ core CPUs or servers. |\n\n## When to Adjust\n\n- **Lower threads** (4-8): If the CPU is overheating or other applications are lagging.\n- **Default threads** (num_cores - 1): For best performance without starving other processes.\n- **Higher threads** (16+): If you have a very powerful CPU and want maximum speed.\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **GPU offload**: If you offload most layers to GPU, CPU threads matter less. You can reduce threads to 4-6.\n- **Diminishing returns**: Going beyond num_cores - 1 usually doesn't help much and can hurt due to thread overhead.\n\n<info>\nThe default of 11 assumes a 12-core CPU. On an 8-core CPU, the auto-detect would set it to 7. You can override manually if needed.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'batch-size',
            title: 'Batch Size',
            content: "# Batch Size\n\n**Slider**: 1 to 4096 | **Step**: 1 | **Default**: 512\n\nBatch Size controls how many tokens the model processes in parallel during a single forward pass. Higher batch size = faster generation but significantly more RAM/VRAM usage.\n\n## How It Works\n\nInstead of processing one token at a time, the model processes N tokens together. This amortizes the cost of matrix operations across multiple tokens.\n\n## What the Numbers Mean\n\n| Value | Effect | VRAM Impact |\n|-------|--------|------------|\n| **1** | Slowest. Processes one token per pass. Lowest RAM. | Minimal |\n| **128** | Slow. Good for memory-constrained systems. | Low |\n| **512** | Default. Good balance for most systems. | Medium |\n| **1024** | Fast. Requires decent VRAM/RAM. | High |\n| **4096** | Maximum. Very fast but memory-intensive. | Very High |\n\n## When to Adjust\n\n- **Batch size = 128-256**: On systems with < 8 GB RAM or < 4 GB VRAM.\n- **Batch size = 512**: Default for most users with 16 GB RAM and 6+ GB VRAM.\n- **Batch size = 1024-2048**: On systems with 32+ GB RAM and 12+ GB VRAM.\n- **Batch size = 4096**: For powerful servers with 64+ GB RAM and 24+ GB VRAM.\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Too high for RAM**: Setting batch size too high will cause out-of-memory errors and crash the backend.\n- **GPU offload interaction**: With high GPU offload, you can use higher batch sizes because GPU memory is faster and more abundant.\n- **Diminishing returns**: Beyond 1024, speed gains are minimal while memory costs explode.\n\n<warning>\nIf you get \"out of memory\" errors, reduce batch size first. This is the most common fix.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'gpu-layers',
            title: 'GPU Offload Layers',
            content: "# GPU Offload Layers\n\n**Slider**: 0 to 100 | **Step**: 1 | **Default**: 0 (auto-managed by gollama)\n\nGPU Offload Layers controls how many model layers are moved to GPU memory. GPU inference is typically 3-10x faster than CPU.\n\n## How It Works\n\nThe model is composed of layers (e.g., 32 layers for a 7B model). Each layer can run on CPU or GPU. Offloading more layers to GPU increases speed but requires more VRAM.\n\n## What the Numbers Mean\n\n| Value | Effect | VRAM Required (7B model) |\n|-------|--------|--------------------------|\n| **0** | CPU only. Slowest. No VRAM. | 0 GB |\n| **10** | Partial offload. Some speedup. | ~2 GB |\n| **20** | Moderate offload. Noticeable speedup. | ~4 GB |\n| **35** | Full 7B model offload. Max speed. | ~6 GB |\n| **100** | All layers. Same as layer count. | Depends on model |\n\n## When to Adjust\n\n- **0 layers**: If you have no GPU or VRAM is needed for other tasks.\n- **20-30 layers**: If you have 4-8 GB VRAM and want a balance.\n- **Full offload (layer count)**: If you have enough VRAM for the entire model. This is ideal for speed.\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Default is 0**: The gollama backend auto-manages this. Setting it manually overrides auto-detection.\n- **Model size matters**: A 70B model requires ~40 GB VRAM for full offload. Most consumer GPUs can't handle this.\n- **Mixed precision**: Some GPUs support quantized models in less VRAM. Check your GPU's capabilities.\n\n<info>\nTo find the layer count for your model, check the GGUF metadata or use the gollama CLI. A 7B model typically has 32-35 layers. A 13B model has 40-44 layers.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'kv-cache-size',
            title: 'KV Cache Size',
            content: "# KV Cache Size\n\n**Slider**: 512 to 32768 | **Step**: 512 | **Default**: 4096\n\nKV Cache Size controls how many tokens of context are cached in memory before being evicted. The KV cache stores key-value pairs from previous tokens to avoid recomputing them.\n\n## How It Works\n\nWhen the model processes a token, it stores the attention keys and values in the KV cache. On the next token, it can reuse this cache instead of recomputing from scratch. This makes generation much faster for long contexts.\n\n## What the Numbers Mean\n\n| Value | Effect | Memory Impact |\n|-------|--------|---------------|\n| **512** | Small cache. Frequent evictions. Low memory. | ~100 MB |\n| **2048** | Medium cache. Good for short chats. | ~400 MB |\n| **4096** | Default. Balanced for most use cases. | ~800 MB |\n| **8192** | Large cache. Good for long stories. | ~1.6 GB |\n| **32768** | Maximum. For very long contexts. | ~6.4 GB |\n\n## When to Adjust\n\n- **2048-4096**: For typical chat and short creative writing.\n- **8192-16384**: For long-form storytelling, novels, or complex roleplay.\n- **32768**: For document analysis, code generation with large context, or research.\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Cannot exceed context window**: KV cache size should be ≤ Max Context Window. Setting it higher wastes memory.\n- **Memory tradeoff**: Larger KV cache = faster but uses more RAM/VRAM. If you're tight on memory, reduce this.\n\n<info>\nKV cache size is per chat session. If you have multiple chats open, each has its own cache. Be mindful of total memory usage.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'kv-cache-type',
            title: 'KV Cache Type',
            content: "# KV Cache Type\n\n**Select**: Standard, Flash-Attention, Paged | **Default**: Standard\n\nKV Cache Type determines how the KV cache is stored and managed in memory. Different types offer tradeoffs between speed, memory efficiency, and compatibility.\n\n## What the Values Mean\n\n| Type | Description | Speed | Memory | Compatibility |\n|------|-------------|-------|--------|---------------|\n| **Standard** | Traditional KV cache. | Baseline | Baseline | Universal |\n| **Flash-Attention** | Optimized attention kernel. | 2-3x faster | Same | Modern GPUs (Ampere+) |\n| **Paged** | Memory-mapped paging. | Slower | Lower | Very long contexts |\n\n## When to Use Each\n\n- **Standard**: Default. Works everywhere. Good for older GPUs and CPU-only setups.\n- **Flash-Attention**: If you have a modern NVIDIA GPU (RTX 30xx/40xx, A100). Significantly faster for long contexts.\n- **Paged**: If you're working with very long contexts (32k+ tokens) and need memory efficiency.\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Flash-Attention requires GPU**: This only works on GPU. If you're CPU-only, it falls back to standard.\n- **Flash-Attention compatibility**: Requires CUDA 11.8+ and modern GPU architecture. Older GPUs may not support it.\n- **Paged overhead**: Paged KV cache has CPU overhead. Use it only for memory-constrained long-context scenarios.\n\n<warning>\nIf Flash-Attention causes errors or crashes, switch back to Standard. Some GPU/driver combinations don't support it.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'rope-scaling-mode',
            title: 'Rope Scaling Mode',
            content: "# Rope Scaling Mode\n\n**Select**: Auto, None, Linear, Dynamic, YARN | **Default**: Auto\n\nRoPE (Rotary Position Embedding) Scaling extends the model's usable context window beyond its trained limit. Different models use different scaling methods.\n\n## How It Works\n\nModels are trained on a fixed context window (e.g., 2048 tokens). RoPE scaling mathematically stretches the position embeddings to allow longer contexts without retraining.\n\n## What the Values Mean\n\n| Mode | Description | Best For |\n|------|-------------|----------|\n| **Auto** | Detected from GGUF metadata. | Most models. |\n| **None** | No scaling. Use trained limit. | Short contexts, legacy models. |\n| **Linear** | Simple multiplication. | Older models (LLaMA 1). |\n| **Dynamic** | Adaptive per-head scaling. | LLaMA 2/3, modern models. |\n| **YARN** | Yet Another RoPE extension. | Very long contexts (16k+). |\n\n## When to Override\n\n- **Keep Auto**: For most models. The GGUF metadata specifies the correct scaling mode.\n- **Override to None**: If you're using the model within its trained context limit and want maximum accuracy.\n- **Override to YARN**: If you need very long contexts (32k+) and the model supports it.\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Rope Factor interaction**: Rope Factor (below) controls HOW MUCH scaling happens. Mode controls HOW scaling happens.\n- **Model-specific**: Not all models support all modes. Using the wrong mode degrades quality.\n- **Accuracy tradeoff**: RoPE scaling is an approximation. Quality degrades slightly as you exceed the trained limit.\n\n<info>\nLLaMA 3 uses Dynamic scaling by default. LLaMA 2 uses Linear. LFM uses Linear. Auto-detection handles this correctly.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'rope-factor',
            title: 'Rope Factor',
            content: "# Rope Factor\n\n**Slider**: 1.0 to 8.0 | **Step**: 0.1 | **Default**: 1.0\n\nRope Factor controls HOW AGGRESSIVELY the context window is extended. It multiplies the effective context length by this factor.\n\n## How It Works\n\nIf a model is trained on 2048 tokens and Rope Factor = 2.0:\n- Effective context = 2048 × 2.0 = 4096 tokens\n- The model can handle twice its trained limit\n\n## What the Numbers Mean\n\n| Value | Effect | Example (2048 trained) |\n|-------|--------|----------------------|\n| **1.0** | No extension. Use trained limit. | 2048 tokens |\n| **2.0** | 2x extension. | 4096 tokens |\n| **4.0** | 4x extension. | 8192 tokens |\n| **8.0** | 8x extension. | 16384 tokens |\n\n## When to Adjust\n\n- **1.0 (no scaling)**: If you stay within the model's trained limit. Best accuracy.\n- **2.0-4.0**: Moderate extension. Good for long stories and complex roleplay.\n- **6.0-8.0**: Aggressive extension. For very long contexts (document analysis, codebases).\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Quality degradation**: Higher factors = more approximation error. Quality drops as you exceed the trained limit.\n- **Model capability**: Some models handle scaling better than others. LLaMA 3 handles 8x well. Older models may struggle at 2x.\n- **Memory impact**: Longer context requires larger KV cache. Increase KV Cache Size accordingly.\n\n<warning>\nDon't set Rope Factor higher than needed. If you only need 4k context, use factor 2.0 on a 2k model, not 8.0.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'tensor-split',
            title: 'Tensor Split',
            content: "# Tensor Split\n\n**Slider**: 0 to 100 | **Step**: 1 | **Default**: 0\n\nTensor Split controls how model layers are distributed across multiple GPUs in a multi-GPU setup. This is for users with 2+ GPUs.\n\n## How It Works\n\nThe model layers are split between GPUs based on the percentage. Each GPU handles its portion, and results are combined.\n\n## What the Numbers Mean\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **0** | Single GPU or CPU. No splitting. |\n| **50** | 50% of layers on GPU 0, 50% on GPU 1. Balanced split. |\n| **30** | 30% on GPU 0, 70% on GPU 1. Useful if GPU 1 is larger. |\n| **100** | All layers on GPU 0. Same as 0. |\n\n## When to Use\n\n- **0 (default)**: If you have only one GPU or are using CPU.\n- **50**: If you have two equal GPUs and want to maximize throughput.\n- **Custom**: If you have asymmetric GPUs (e.g., one 12 GB, one 24 GB). Set split to favor the larger GPU.\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Requires multi-GPU**: This only works if you have multiple GPUs. On single-GPU systems, it has no effect.\n- **PCIe bottleneck**: Splitting across GPUs connected via PCIe introduces communication overhead. Sometimes single GPU is faster despite lower VRAM.\n- **gollama auto**: The backend may auto-manage this. Manual override is for advanced users.\n\n<info>\nTensor split is for **same-model multi-GPU**, not model sharding. For running multiple models simultaneously, use separate backend instances.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'max-context',
            title: 'Max Context Window',
            content: "# Max Context Window\n\n**Slider**: 1024 to 262144 | **Step**: 1024 | **Default**: 4096\n\nMax Context Window is the maximum number of tokens the model can consider in a single generation. This includes your messages, system prompt, character data, lorebook entries, and chat history.\n\n## How It Works\n\nWhen the context exceeds this limit, the oldest tokens are evicted. The model \"forgets\" earlier parts of the conversation.\n\n## What the Numbers Mean\n\n| Value | Effect | Equivalent Text |\n|-------|--------|-----------------|\n| **1024** | Very short. ~1 page of text. | Quick exchanges. |\n| **4096** | Default. ~3 pages of text. | Typical chat session. |\n| **8192** | Long. ~6 pages of text. | Extended roleplay. |\n| **16384** | Very long. ~12 pages of text. | Novels, documents. |\n| **32768** | Maximum. ~24 pages of text. | Codebases, research. |\n| **262144** | Extreme. ~200 pages of text. | Massive documents. |\n\n## When to Adjust\n\n- **2048-4096**: For chat, creative writing, roleplay.\n- **8192-16384**: For long-form storytelling, document analysis.\n- **32768+**: For code generation with large context, research papers, books.\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Memory impact**: Larger context requires more RAM/VRAM. 16k context needs ~4x the memory of 4k.\n- **Model limit**: Cannot exceed the model's trained limit without RoPE scaling.\n- **Rollover interaction**: When context exceeds this limit, Context Rollover Mode determines what gets evicted.\n\n<warning>\nDon't set this higher than you need. 16k context uses 4x the memory of 4k but most chats never need that much.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'context-rollover-mode',
            title: 'Context Rollover Mode',
            content: "# Context Rollover Mode\n\n**Select**: Memory-Guided, Standard, Sliding Window | **Default**: Memory-Guided\n\nContext Rollover Mode determines HOW the system evicts old tokens when the context window is exceeded.\n\n## What the Values Mean\n\n| Mode | Description | Best For |\n|------|-------------|----------|\n| **Memory-Guided** | HOWL's memory system selects what to keep based on importance. | Roleplay, stories. |\n| **Standard** | Evict the oldest tokens first (FIFO). | Chat, simple use cases. |\n| **Sliding Window** | Keep only the most recent N tokens. | Real-time applications. |\n\n## How Each Mode Works\n\n**Memory-Guided** (HOWL default):\n- The memory system scores tokens based on importance\n- Character names, recent dialogue, and key events are prioritized\n- Less important filler is evicted first\n- Requires Memory Integration enabled\n\n**Standard** (FIFO):\n- Oldest tokens are evicted first\n- Simple and predictable\n- May evict important context if it's old\n\n**Sliding Window**:\n- Only the most recent tokens are kept\n- Everything before the window is discarded\n- Good for real-time but bad for long-term memory\n\n## When to Use Each\n\n- **Memory-Guided**: Default for HOWL. Best for immersive roleplay and stories.\n- **Standard**: If you don't want to use the memory system or want simple FIFO behavior.\n- **Sliding Window**: For real-time chatbots where only recent context matters.\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Memory Integration dependency**: Memory-Guided only works if Memory Integration is enabled.\n- **Memory system required**: Memory-Guided requires the memory backend to be running.\n\n<info>\nMemory-Guided is HOWL's specialty. It's why HOWL can maintain coherent long-form stories better than other chat interfaces.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'memory-integration',
            title: 'Memory Integration',
            content: "# Memory Integration\n\n**Toggle**: Enabled by default | **Default**: true\n\nMemory Integration enables HOWL's memory system to automatically inject relevant memories into the context after rollover. This is critical for maintaining long-term coherence.\n\n## How It Works\n\nWhen the context window is exceeded and tokens are evicted:\n1. The memory system analyzes the current situation\n2. It retrieves relevant memories (character traits, past events, world details)\n3. These memories are injected back into the context\n4. The model \"remembers\" important details even after they were evicted\n\n## What It Does\n\n- **Character consistency**: Remembers character names, personalities, relationships\n- **World continuity**: Remembers locations, lore, world rules\n- **Event tracking**: Remembers past events and their consequences\n- **Lorebook injection**: Automatically includes relevant lorebook entries\n\n## When to Disable\n\n- **Keep enabled**: Default. Recommended for all HOWL use cases.\n- **Disable**: If you're using HOWL as a simple chat interface and don't need memory features.\n- **Disable**: If the memory backend is causing performance issues.\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Required for Memory-Guided rollover**: If disabled, Memory-Guided rollover falls back to Standard.\n- **Performance impact**: Memory retrieval adds a small delay (~50-200ms) during context rollover.\n- **Memory backend required**: Requires the memory system to be running.\n\n<info>\nMemory Integration is one of HOWL's core features. It's what makes long-form roleplay and storytelling possible without the model forgetting important details.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'system-prompt-cache',
            title: 'System Prompt Cache',
            content: "# System Prompt Cache\n\n**Toggle**: Enabled by default | **Default**: true\n\nSystem Prompt Cache keeps the system prompt pinned in the KV cache so it doesn't need to be reprocessed on every generation. This improves performance for long system prompts.\n\n## How It Works\n\nThe system prompt (character description, world rules, instructions) is processed once and cached. On subsequent generations:\n- The cached system prompt is reused\n- Only new messages are processed\n- Faster generation and lower CPU/GPU usage\n\n## What It Does\n\n- **Speed**: Reduces processing time for long system prompts\n- **Memory**: Uses slightly more KV cache memory\n- **Consistency**: Ensures the system prompt is always available\n\n## When to Disable\n\n- **Keep enabled**: Default. Recommended for most users.\n- **Disable**: If you're extremely tight on KV cache memory.\n- **Disable**: If you frequently change the system prompt between generations.\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Memory tradeoff**: Caching uses more KV cache memory. If you're hitting memory limits, disable this.\n- **Dynamic prompts**: If you modify the system prompt frequently (e.g., per-message instructions), caching may not help much.\n\n<info>\nSystem Prompt Cache is most beneficial for character systems with long, detailed descriptions (500+ tokens).\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'prompt-template',
            title: 'Prompt Template',
            content: "# Prompt Template\n\n**Select**: Auto, LLaMA, Mistral, ChatML, Alpaca, Vicuna, LFM, Custom (Jinja) | **Default**: Auto\n\nPrompt Template defines the chat format — how user messages, assistant messages, and system prompts are formatted for the model.\n\n## How It Works\n\nDifferent models expect different chat formats:\n- **ChatML**: \\`<|im_start|>user\\nHello<|im_end|>\\n<|im_start|>assistant\\n...\\`\n- **LLaMA**: `\\[INST] Hello [/INST]\\`\n- **Mistral**: `<s>\\[INST] Hello [/INST]\\`\n\nThe template wraps your messages in the correct format for the model.\n\n## What the Values Mean\n\n| Template | Used By | Format |\n|----------|---------|--------|\n| **Auto** | Auto-detected from GGUF | Varies |\n| **LLaMA** | LLaMA/LLaMA 2 | \\`[INST] ... [/INST]\\` |\n| **Mistral** | Mistral/Mixtral | \\`<s>[INST] ... [/INST]\\` |\n| **ChatML** | Many modern models | `\\<|im_start|> ... <|im_end|>` |\n| **Alpaca** | Legacy models | \\`### User: ... ### Assistant:\\` |\n| **Vicuna** | Vicuna | `\\USER: ... ASSISTANT:\\` |\n| **LFM** | LLaMA For Math | LFM-specific |\n| **Custom (Jinja)** | Manual override | Jinja2 template |\n\n## When to Override\n\n- **Keep Auto**: Default. Detected from GGUF metadata.\n- **Override**: If the model was converted with incorrect metadata.\n- **Override**: If you're using a custom fine-tune that requires a specific format.\n- **Custom Jinja**: If you need full manual control over the format.\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Wrong format = garbage**: Using the wrong template causes the model to produce nonsensical output.\n- **Custom Jinja complexity**: Custom Jinja templates require knowledge of Jinja2 syntax and the model's expected format.\n\n<info>\nChatML is the modern standard for most 2024+ models. Auto-detection handles this correctly.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'custom-jinja-template',
            title: 'Custom Jinja Template',
            content: "# Custom Jinja Template\n\n**Textarea**: Full manual Jinja2 template | **Default**: empty\n\nCustom Jinja Template allows you to manually override the prompt template using Jinja2 templating syntax. This is for advanced users who need complete control.\n\n## How It Works\n\nJinja2 templates use placeholders like \\`{{messages}}\\` and \\`{{system_prompt}}\\` to dynamically build the formatted prompt.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- **Keep empty**: Default. Use the built-in templates or Auto-detection.\n- **Custom**: If you're using a model with a non-standard format not covered by built-in templates.\n- **Custom**: If you need to inject custom tokens, separators, or formatting.\n\n## Example Template\n\n\\`\\`\\`jinja\n{% for message in messages %}\n{{ message.role | upper }}: {{ message.content }}\n{% endfor %}\nASSISTANT:\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Requires Prompt Template = Custom (Jinja)**: This field only has effect when Prompt Template is set to \"Custom (Jinja)\".\n- **Complexity**: Jinja2 syntax can be complex. Test your template carefully.\n- **Model-specific**: Templates are model-specific. A template for one model won't work for another.\n\n<warning>\nOnly use Custom Jinja if you know what you're doing. Wrong templates will break generation.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'system-prompt-override',
            title: 'System Prompt Override',
            content: "# System Prompt Override\n\n**Textarea**: Full manual system prompt | **Default**: empty\n\nSystem Prompt Override completely replaces the model's default system prompt with your custom instructions.\n\n## How It Works\n\nThe system prompt is the initial instruction that tells the model how to behave. By default, HOWL constructs this from:\n- Character description\n- World rules\n- Scenario instructions\n- User preferences\n\nSystem Prompt Override replaces all of this with your custom text.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- **Keep empty**: Default. Let HOWL build the system prompt from character/world data.\n- **Custom**: If you want complete control over the model's behavior.\n- **Custom**: If you're using HOWL for a specific task (coding, analysis) and don't need character/world context.\n\n## Example\n\n\\`\\`\\`\nYou are a helpful coding assistant. Provide clear, concise code examples. Explain your reasoning step by step.\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Overrides character/world**: This replaces the character description and world rules. The model won't know about your character or world.\n- **HOWL-specific**: System Prompt Override is for HOWL's context building. Don't use this to set sampler or model settings.\n\n<info>\nSystem Prompt Override is useful for turning HOWL into a general-purpose assistant for coding, analysis, or other tasks.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'user-prompt-prefix',
            title: 'User Prompt Prefix',
            content: "# User Prompt Prefix\n\n**Text Input**: Prefix added before every user message | **Default**: empty\n\nUser Prompt Prefix adds a fixed string before each of your messages in the context.\n\n## How It Works\n\nYour message: *\"Hello, how are you?\"*\nWith prefix: `\"You: Hello, how are you?\"`\n\n## When to Use\n\n- **Keep empty**: Default. No prefix.\n- **Custom**: If the model expects a specific user prefix format.\n- **Custom**: For roleplay formats (e.g., \"User: \" or \"You: \").\n\n## Example\n\nSet prefix to: `You: `\nResult: `You: Hello, how are you?`\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Template interaction**: The prompt template already handles formatting. User Prefix is an additional layer.\n- **Redundant with template**: If the template already adds \"User: \", don't add it again here.\n\n<info>\nMost users don't need this. The Prompt Template handles formatting automatically.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'assistant-prompt-prefix',
            title: 'Assistant Prompt Prefix',
            content: "# Assistant Prompt Prefix\n\n**Text Input**: Prefix added before every assistant message | **Default**: empty\n\nAssistant Prompt Prefix adds a fixed string before each AI response in the context.\n\n## How It Works\n\nAI response: *\"I'm doing well, thank you!\"*\nWith prefix: `\"Assistant: I'm doing well, thank you!\"`\n\n## When to Use\n\n- **Keep empty**: Default. No prefix.\n- **Custom**: If the model expects a specific assistant prefix format.\n- **Custom**: For roleplay formats (e.g., \"Assistant: \" or \"AI: \").\n\n## Example\n\nSet prefix to: `Assistant: `\nResult: `Assistant: I'm doing well, thank you!`\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Template interaction**: The prompt template already handles formatting. Assistant Prefix is an additional layer.\n- **Redundant with template**: If the template already adds \"Assistant: \", don't add it again here.\n\n<info>\nMost users don't need this. The Prompt Template handles formatting automatically.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'stop-sequences',
            title: 'Stop Sequences',
            content: "# Stop Sequences\n\n**Text Input**: Comma-separated strings that halt generation | **Default**: empty (auto)\n\nStop Sequences define specific strings that, when generated, cause the model to immediately stop generating.\n\n## How It Works\n\nIf the model generates one of the stop sequences, generation halts immediately. This is useful for controlling output format.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- **Keep empty (auto)**: Default. The model uses its built-in stop tokens.\n- **Custom**: If the model is generating too much text or continuing when it shouldn't.\n- **Custom**: For structured output (e.g., stop at \"\\\\n\\\\n\" to force paragraph breaks).\n\n## Examples\n\n- Stop at: \\`\\\\n\\\\n\\` → Model stops at double newline (paragraphs)\n- Stop at: \\`\\\\nUser:\\` → Model stops when it would start simulating user\n- Stop at: \\`</s>\\` → Model stops at special token\n- Stop at: \\`###\\` → Model stops at markdown header\n\nMultiple sequences: `\\\\n\\\\n,\\\\nUser:,</s>`\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Too restrictive**: If stop sequences are too common, the model may stop unexpectedly early.\n- **Format**: Must be comma-separated. No spaces after commas for exact matching.\n\n<warning>\nStop sequences are literal string matches. Case-sensitive and exact. Test your sequences carefully.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'tokenizer-override',
            title: 'Tokenizer Override',
            content: "# Tokenizer Override\n\n**Select**: Auto, SentencePiece, TikToken, Custom | **Default**: Auto\n\nTokenizer Override forces the use of a specific tokenizer. This is for models with mismatched or incorrect tokenizer metadata.\n\n## How It Works\n\nThe tokenizer converts text to tokens. Different model families use different tokenizers:\n- **SentencePiece**: LLaMA, Mistral, most GGUF models\n- **TikToken**: GPT-style models\n- **Custom**: Manual tokenizer specification\n\n## What the Values Mean\n\n| Value | Use For |\n|-------|---------|\n| **Auto** | Detected from GGUF metadata. |\n| **SentencePiece** | Most GGUF models. |\n| **TikToken** | OpenAI-style models. |\n| **Custom** | Manual tokenizer path. |\n\n## When to Override\n\n- **Keep Auto**: Default. Detected from GGUF metadata.\n- **Override**: If the model was converted with incorrect tokenizer metadata.\n- **Override**: If you're using a custom tokenizer not covered by built-in options.\n\n## Conflicts & Warnings\n\n- **Wrong tokenizer = garbage**: Using the wrong tokenizer causes the model to produce nonsense.\n- **Model-specific**: Tokenizers are model-specific. Don't mix and match.\n\n<warning>\nOnly override if you know the tokenizer metadata is wrong. Incorrect tokenizer selection breaks generation completely.\n</warning>"
        }
    ]
};
